course: dictionary
lesson: 16138
title: Wholesale and retail
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Wholesale means selling goods in large amounts, usually to shops and businesses rather than to the people who use them. «The shop bought 500 pencils wholesale from the factory.» The shop bought lots at once, so each pencil cost less.
explain: The shop then sells the pencils one at a time to customers, at a higher price. That higher price is the retail price. The gap between wholesale and retail prices is how shops make a profit.
explain: Wholesale can also describe something huge and sweeping, often bad. «The storm caused wholesale destruction across the town.» Here it means on a very large scale, with damage everywhere. Check the clues: is the sentence about buying in bulk, or about something enormous?
fact: Wholesale means selling goods in large amounts to shops and businesses.
fact: Retail means selling goods one at a time to the public.
fact: A wholesale price is the lower price that shops pay when they buy goods in bulk.
fact: «Wholesale» can also mean done on a very large scale, often too much and without care, as in «wholesale changes».
fact: Wholesale can be an adverb too: «The shop bought the stock wholesale.»
exercise: «The bakery buys flour wholesale.» What does wholesale mean here? | Given away for free | Very slowly and carefully | Only one bag at a time, at the till | *In large amounts, sold to businesses at a lower price
exercise: Which price do shops pay when they buy goods in large amounts? | The price of a single biscuit in a café | The retail price | *The wholesale price | The price on the till receipt
exercise: «The new rules brought wholesale changes to the school.» What does wholesale suggest? | Small changes in one room | *Huge, sweeping changes | Changes made only by a wholesaler | Quiet, careful changes
test: «The shop sells toys at retail prices, but it buys them wholesale.» Which price is higher? | Neither, because toys are free | Wholesale | They are always the same | *Retail
test: «The flood caused wholesale damage to homes.» Does wholesale suggest small or large damage? | *Large damage, spread over many places | Small damage to one door | No damage at all | Damage only to the roof
exam: «A factory makes cups, a wholesaler buys thousands of them, and a shop sells them one at a time.» Who buys in large amounts? | The factory worker | *The wholesaler | The shop's cat | The customer at the shop
